{"name":"Copper Beech","hex":"#b1715a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"copper beech","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A tall type of beech, Fagus sylvatica atropurpurea, with smooth bark and reddish-brown to purple leaves."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/copper_beech"}],"entries":[{"title":"Copper Beech Manor","blocks":[{"text":"Copper Beech Manor is a historic building at 17 Market Street in Lewisburg, Pennsylvania, which is now a residence."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copper_Beech_Manor"},{"title":"Fagus sylvatica","blocks":[{"text":"Fagus sylvatica, the European beech or common beech, is a species of tree in the flowering plant family Fagaceae. It can grow to a height of more than 40 m and has smooth silvery-grey bark, large leaf area, and a short trunk with low branches. It is native to much of Europe, growing in humid climates."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Fagus-sylvatica-cansiglio-forest-italy.jpg/330px-Fagus-sylvatica-cansiglio-forest-italy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fagus_sylvatica"},{"title":"Copper","blocks":[{"text":"Copper is a chemical element; it has symbol Cu and atomic number 29. It is a soft, malleable, and ductile metal with very high thermal and electrical conductivity. A freshly exposed surface of pure copper has a pinkish-orange color. Copper is used as a conductor of heat and electricity, as a building material, and as a constituent of various metal alloys, such as sterling silver used in jewelry, cupronickel used to make marine hardware and coins, and constantan used in strain gauges and thermocouples for temperature measurement."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/NatCopper.jpg/330px-NatCopper.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copper"},{"title":"Beech","blocks":[{"text":"Beech is a genus of deciduous trees in the family Fagaceae, native to subtropical and temperate Eurasia and North America. There are 14 accepted species in two distinct subgenera, Englerianae Denk & G.W.Grimm and Fagus. The subgenus Englerianae is found only in East Asia, distinctive for its low branches, often made up of several major trunks with yellowish bark. The better known species of subgenus Fagus are native to Europe, western and eastern Asia and eastern North America."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Beech-tree.JPG/330px-Beech-tree.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beech"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Entzia_-_Brotes_de_haya_01.jpg/250px-Entzia_-_Brotes_de_haya_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Entzia_-_Brotes_de_haya_01.jpg/330px-Entzia_-_Brotes_de_haya_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Entzia_-_Brotes_de_haya_01.jpg/250px-Entzia_-_Brotes_de_haya_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEntzia_-_Brotes_de_haya_01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Beuk_%28Fagus_sylvatica%29%2C_zwellende_bladknop._24-04-2022_%28d.j.b.%29.jpg/120px-Beuk_%28Fagus_sylvatica%29%2C_zwellende_bladknop._24-04-2022_%28d.j.b.%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Beuk_%28Fagus_sylvatica%29%2C_zwellende_bladknop._24-04-2022_%28d.j.b.%29.jpg/250px-Beuk_%28Fagus_sylvatica%29%2C_zwellende_bladknop._24-04-2022_%28d.j.b.%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign